# XSH pthread_cleanup_pop() and pthread_cleanup_push()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Every push is paired with a pop in the same lexical scope, as the page
# requires of applications, and no block is left early.

assertion  XSH.pthread_cleanup_pop.001
anchor     functions/pthread_cleanup_pop.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       After pushing handlers with arguments 11, 22, 33, the calls
           pthread_cleanup_pop(1), pthread_cleanup_pop(0) and
           pthread_cleanup_pop(7) run the handler with 33 and then 11
           only.
note       On a system whose pthread_cleanup_pop() is a macro that calls
           the handler inline, the faults in what it runs cannot be
           planted; the test is then reviewed by hand.
test       t/001-pop.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cleanup_pop.002
anchor     functions/pthread_cleanup_pop.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.3
class      required
since      2008
says       A thread that pushed handlers 1 and 2, popped 2 with
           pthread_cleanup_pop(0), pushed 3 and 4 and called
           pthread_exit() runs them as 4, 3, 1, and pthread_join() gives
           the value passed to pthread_exit().
test       t/002-exit.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cleanup_pop.003
anchor     functions/pthread_cleanup_pop.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.3
class      required
since      2008
says       A thread canceled with two nested handlers pushed, whose
           arguments point to volatile automatic objects of the pushed
           blocks (assigned 10 and 20 after the pushes), runs the inner
           handler and then the outer, once each; each handler can still
           read its object's storage (20, then 10).
note       The objects are volatile: the macros may use setjmp(), after
           which ISO C leaves non-volatile automatic objects modified
           later indeterminate; 2.9.5.3 promises only that the storage
           remains valid.
test       t/003-cancel.c
